is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Layer-2/Layer-3 connectivity between Laptop2 (172.16.10.12 on switch1 fa3) and Laptop3 (172.16.10.13 on switch1 fa4) is healthy. ICMP tests succeed both directions with sub‑millisecond RTTs (L2→L3 OK: laptop2→laptop3 0.79 ms avg; laptop3→laptop2 0.51 ms avg). ARP resolution is present on both ends (laptop2 sees 172.16.10.13 at 6a:6a:7d:f8:6c:8a; laptop3 sees 172.16.10.12 at 9e:9d:74:af:d3:ee), and link state is up at full duplex with zero errors/drops. This rules out a dead port or a switching fault on switch1.

What is failing is the application layer “file sharing” service. On both laptops there are no servers listening on any non-loopback address (netstat shows only 127.0.0.11 listeners on both laptop2 and laptop3; no SMB/NFS/SSH/HTTP shares are exposed). With no service bound to 0.0.0.0/172.16.10.x, peer discovery and file transfers will fail even though the network path is fine.

Root cause: The file-sharing daemon(s) on the laptops are not running or not bound to the LAN interface; this is not a switch or port issue.

Corrective fix: Start/enable the intended sharing service on one or both laptops and ensure it listens on eth0 (172.16.10.x) and is permitted by the local firewall. For a quick workaround, run a temporary server (e.g., on the sender: python3 -m http.server 8000; on the other: curl or browser to http://172.16.10.x:8000). If using SMB/NFS/SSH, enable and start the respective service and open the ports. No switch restart is required. 
=== END ===